Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex re(R"(<a[^>]*>");
    std::regex_replace(html, re, "<a href=\"https://www.example.com\">");
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a [markdown text](https://www.example.com)";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace any `<a>` tags in the markdown text with an `<a>` tag with an `href` attribute set to "https://www.example.com". The regular expression `R"(<a[^>]*>"` matches any `<a>` tag with any characters before the `>` symbol. The `std::regex_replace` function is used to replace all matches in the markdown text with the specified HTML tag. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function and output the result.

